      "raw_response": "To determine which option is correct, we need to consider the available data on working hours across different countries. Generally, studies on working hours often compare countries within Europe, North America, and some Asian nations. Reliable sources such as the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD) provide data on average weekly hours worked.\n\nA. Americans work longer hours than Mexicans: This is a plausible comparison given that the United States is a highly industrialized country with significant income inequality, while Mexico has lower per capita income and more informal employment. According to OECD data, American workers work the longest weekly hours on average.\n\nB. Mexicans work less than Chinese: This is not supported by the available data. OECD reports show that Mexicans work longer hours than Chinese.\n\nC. Mexico works less than Japan: Japan is known for having one of the longest working hours, so this comparison is unlikely.\n\nD. The United States: This is a part of option A and is a plausible answer.\n\nE. Canadians work harder than Mexicans: While Canada has a higher standard of living, the OECD data suggests that Mexicans work longer hours.\n\nF. Mexicans have some of the highest hours but Cambodia might be higher: This is contradictory to A and B, and there is no substantial data supporting this claim.\n\nBased on these considerations, the most plausible option is:\n\nANSWER: A",
